invest in those kinds  of interventions mayor ricardo a  question for you from rhonda schwartz  rhonda  gun rights groups have said this is  absolutely preposterous idea and they're  promising to sue you mayor what do you  make of their reaction and how are you  going to go up against that  well the reaction is more or less what  we anticipated  we've had a lawsuit already  we know that to file a lawsuit in this  country means exercising your seventh  amendment rights and you have to pay a  fee at the courthouse steps to file that  complaint  similarly for  a newspaper to publish a story about  this event  many of those newspapers pay taxes and  that has been upheld  by the supreme court and the mere fact  that it's a constitutional right doesn't  mean it can't be regulated or there  can't be a fee that attaches the  question is whether we're creating  barriers that are uh too burdensome in  the exercise of that right i'm confident  this is a very very modest uh fee and  and certainly not gonna impair anyone  from exercising the constitutional  rights in this country you know we we  certainly have a second amendment that  protects the right of every american to  own and possess a gun  but it doesn't require every taxpayer to  subsidize  the exercise of that right and right now  that's exactly what we're doing in  california we're spending 1.4 billion  dollars on the public cost of responding  to gun harm  mr mayor if you are successful here this  uphold is upheld by the courts this  could be a template for the rest of the  country  well we hope that other cities and  states will  in fact engage in this kind of  policy uh we want insurance companies to  get involved because we see how  effective that has been in reducing  deaths for example with regard to auto  accidents over five decades we've seen  eighty percent drop in auto related  fatalities because  people have been encouraged to buy cars  with analog brakes or airbags and we  have  good driver safety discounts uh you know  through  utilizing uh  risk adjusted premiums insurance  companies can encourage safer behavior  and we need that in a country with four  and a half million children that live in  a home where a gun is kept unlocked  and loaded and we know that gun safes  and trigger locks and gun safety classes  could go a long way to making those  homes much safer and preventing a lot  of harm  with so many guns in this country you're  up against a huge task in trying to get  gun safety laws passed  you're right  we have a country with more than 300  million guns and we're not going to  magically make  everyone safe with any ordinance or  statute what we're hoping is to make gun  ownership safer we're not going to take  guns away from anyone no one has to fear  a big bad government getting involved  here what we're trying to do is make  strategic investments with public money  that can resu reduce harm  uh and  encourage the private sector in the form  of insurance companies  to incentivize safer behavior all right  mayor sam ricardo san jose california  thank you so much for being with us here  this evening  it's great to be with you brian thank  you all right  we asked the national association of gun  rights to have someone appear here  tonight with the mayor but we did not  hear back from them well up next this  week's winners and losers two losers in  the media  two very big losers  you're watching brian ross investigates  on the law and crime trial network  time now for this week's winners and  losers in the media as chosen by the  editors of mediaite and we're joined by  aidan mclaughlin who's the  editor-in-chief of media which like the  law and crime trial network is part of  the dan abrams media group and this week  aidan two losers and no winners the  first big loser jeff sucker at cnn out  abruptly resigning what's behind the  scenes  it's been that kind of weak brian um  jeff zucker uh was uh resigned today uh  he was actually pushed out of cnn a  network he has run since 2013. uh now  just to give you a little glimpse inside  cnn jeff sucker is beloved by almost  everyone who works at cnn and you saw a  series of  uh sort of fond notes pour out from  anchors and producers writers at cnn  were really sad to see this turn of  events  now what happened is uh jeff sucker  resigned this morning he announced his  resignation and he said that the reason  he was resigning is because he had a  relationship with allison gollist who is  an evp uh at cnn and is also the chief  marketing officer of the network  now they've had that relationship for uh  more than a year they said now since  they said it started in the pandemic but  they weren't together since uh they both  worked at nbc back in 2012 so the  relationship is has been going on for  quite a while now they said that the  reason that zucker was resigning is  because he did not disclose that he was  in this relationship with alan allison  golis which are supposed to do because  they're colleagues um now that was the  reason he said he was resigning i know  people at cnn uh have issues with that  allison camerota who's a host on the  network  she said she didn't understand why he  was resigning over a consensual  relationship just because he didn't  disclose it to hr um so i suspect  there's there's a little bit more to  this story we know that uh jason keelar  who is the head of warner media which is  cnn's current parent company has issues  his own issues with uh  jeff zucker and that jason killer was  the one who asked for jeff sucker's uh  resignation and said that he would be  terminated if jeff sucker didn't  announce it himself uh that's brian  stelter of cnn who reported that um so i  think i suspect in the coming days we'll  get a little bit more clarity on why  jeff sucker so abruptly resigned um but  it was clearly not his decision to do so  nate this seems to be more fallout from  the firing of chris cuomo by jeff zucker  that's right that's another sort of wild  element of this chris cuomo who was  fired by cnn in december uh because he  was advising his brother uh the former  new york governor on his sexual  harassment scandal um during the  investigation that cnn uh had uh  declared into that uh into that scandal  within its own network uh jeff zucker  was asked by the lawyers that cnn had  hired for that investigation uh whether  or not he was in this relationship with  allison gallis and that's when he  disclosed it and that's when jason  keelar asked for his resignation so it  all trails back to uh andrew cuomo's sex  scandal which is truly like a pretty  remarkable domino effect to have there  quite a blast radius as ben smith of the  new york times said finally let's go to  your second loser whoopi goldberg of  abc's the view who was suspended for two  weeks for these comments about the  holocaust  the holocaust isn't about race  no  no it's not about racism  it's about a different race but it's  it's not about race  it's not about race  because you it's about man's inhumanity  to man well she was widely criticized  for that uh went on the stephen colbert  show and apologized said the same thing  on the view but that wasn't enough for  abc which put her on a two-week  suspension  that's right uh so after those comments  there was a huge public backlash uh  whoopi goldberg issued a pretty lengthy  apology uh then uh the head of the  anti-defamation league appeared on the  view um to basically give the ladies the  view a lesson in uh the holocaust and uh  why it was indeed very much about race  um that still wasn't enough for abc uh  kim godwin who is the current uh  president of the network announced that  she uh the whoopi goldberg was being  suspended for two weeks uh so that she  could learn um i think it was sort of an  odd decision to suspend her for two  weeks is something the network sort of  has to answer for now um i i don't know  that there was that much of a public  demand for it usually in these  si